    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d417">

                <pb facs="0493" n="481" sameAs="frus:frus1944v03/pg481"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 EW/10–3044: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Kennan</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-10-30T11:00:00+03:00">October 30,
                            1944—11 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 30—10:05 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4146. On October 28, immediately after the signing of the Bulgarian armistice
                    agreement (4138, October 28, 6 p.m.<anchor n="29" xml:id="d417-f1"/>), I took
                    the liberty of handing to Mr. Stainov<anchor n="30" xml:id="d417-f2"/> a letter,
                    addressed to the Bulgarian armistice delegation, stating that the United States
                    Government <pb facs="0494" n="482" xml:id="pg482"/> expected the Bulgarian
                    Government to break immediately all relations with Japan. The wording of the
                    letter was the same as that of the letter addressed to the Rumanian armistice
                    delegation on the same subject (Embassy’s 3492, September 14, 7 p.m.<anchor
                        n="31" xml:id="d417-f3"/>). The British Ambassador,<anchor n="32"
                        xml:id="d417-f4"/> acting on instructions, was handing Mr. Stainov a similar
                    letter at that time, and I feared that any delay on our part in doing likewise
                    might invite interpretation.<anchor n="33" xml:id="d417-f5"/>
                </p>
                <p>Sent to the Department as 4146; repeated to AmPolAd,<anchor n="34"
                        xml:id="d417-f6"/> Caserta, as No. 7.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kennan</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d417-f1" n="29" xml:id="d417-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d417-f2" n="30" xml:id="d417-f2-content">
                <p>Petko Stainov, Bulgarian Minister for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d417-f3" n="31" xml:id="d417-f3-content">
                <p>Vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">iv</hi>, section under Rumania entitled
                    “Post-armistice problems of occupation and control of Rumania …”</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d417-f4" n="32" xml:id="d417-f4-content">
                <p>Sir Archibald J. K. Clark Kerr.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d417-f5" n="33" xml:id="d417-f5-content">
                <p>Bulgaria severed relations with Japan as of November 6.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d417-f6" n="34" xml:id="d417-f6-content">
                <p>American Political Adviser; Alexander C. Kirk was Political Adviser on the staff
                    of the Supreme Allied Commander, Mediterranean (SACMED), Gen. Sir Henry Maitland
                    Wilson.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
